 decision with sensitivity analysis", "Human-preference eval pools are not public; LLM-as-judge introduces proxy bias", "Sparse factorial grid yields less precise interaction estimates; interaction-boundary mapping requires the full extension, while the MVD reliably detects the four primary effects and the negation contrast", "All compute/runtime figures are scoping estimates; no arm is run in this design-only task"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-afd78ca1bb76424e9f8c0aa65a6203c0", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-afd78ca1bb76424e9f8c0aa65a6203c0", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"stage": "result", "summary": "Deliver full factorial experiment design for 13B vs 70B comparison", "answer": "# FINAL EXPERIMENT DESIGN\n\n## Question\n> Does increasing model size (13B \u2192 70B) necessarily improve performance, or can insufficient pre-training and a lack of refined alignment negate the benefit of a larger parameter count?\n\n## 1. Rationale\nThe public Llama-2 release does not answer this question because the two sizes are **confounded**: both 13B and 70B were pre-trained on the same 2T-token corpus and run through the same alignment pipeline (SFT + RLHF). Any observed 13B-vs-70B difference therefore mixes three mechanisms: (i) model size/capacity, (ii) pre-training sufficiency relative to that capacity, and (iii) alignment refinement quality. The shipped checkpoints cannot separate them. The experiment below is a **causal factorial design** that varies the three factors independently enough to (a) give a fair, interpretable \"pure size\" estimate at matched data and alignment, and (b) directly test the \"negation\" mechanism \u2014 an under-trained and/or minimally aligned 70B pitted against a well-pre-trained, well-aligned 13B. Every claim in the question is mapped to a specific cell comparison, so the design either supports or rejects the negation hypothesis rather than just re-reporting benchmark differences.\n\n## 2. Design overview \u2014 three factors, three dimensions\n- **S** = size \u2208 {13B, 70B}\n- **P** = pre-training token budget \u2208 {insufficient, reference 2T, extended}\n- **A** = alignment stage \u2208 {base, SFT-only, chat(SFT+RLHF), refined-chat}\n\nStructure: a 2(S) \u00d7 3(P) \u00d7 4(A-stage) grid filled sparsely (\"diagonal + corners\") to keep compute tractable, with a smaller **Minimal Viable Design (MVD)** as the primary deliverable and the fuller grid as the ideal-case extension.\n\n## 3. Fixed task requirements vs. assumptions (stated explicitly)\n**Fixed (non-negotiable, from the task):** design-only; no experiment may be claimed as run; the answer must be interpretable toward the size-vs-sufficiency/alignment question; the deliverable is the design (rationale, controls, analysis, feasibility).\n\n**Assumptions (not facts \u2014 my choices, reviewable):**\n1. **2T tokens is the reference budget.** Both shipped sizes used 2T tokens; 2T defines the \"reference\" cell and \"insufficiency/extended\" are defined relative to it, not to any source-given absolute.\n2. **The alignment pipeline transfers across sizes.** I assume the SFT set, RM architecture/weights, and RLHF procedure (rejection sampling + PPO, separate helpfulness/safety RMs) are comparable at 13B and 70B and separable from size. This is why re-running alignment is necessary rather than trusting released chat checkpoints.\n3. **Released checkpoints stand in for reference cells after an equivalence sanity check.** The 13B@2T@chat, 70B@2T@chat, and base models = released checkpoints, validated by a reproducibility/equivalence check on a standard benchmark subset; only the non-reference arms (extended P, insufficient P, refined A) are actually trained.\n4. **\"Sufficient pre-training\" is operationalized as a pre-registered threshold** \u2014 either loss-plateau (marginal loss decrease < \u03b4 over a fixed eval-token window) or a Chinchilla-style compute budget (70B \u2192 ~1.4T tokens; extended arm targets ~4T to exceed the paper's own \"under-trained\" note). The threshold is fixed in the pre-registration, not post-hoc.\n5. **LLM-as-judge is a proxy** for human preference; human-eval pools are not public, so the design uses a fixed held-out preference pool if available, otherwise a fixed LLM-judge rubric, pre-registered.\n\n## 4. Treatment and control arms\n\n### 4a. Minimal Viable Design (MVD) \u2014 4 arms (lead deliverable)\n| Arm | S | P | A | Label | Purpose |\n|----|----|----|----|----|----|\n| C (control/reference) | 13B | 2T | chat | `13B\u00b72T\u00b7chat` | shipped llama-2-13b-chat; anchors the comparison |\n| T1 | 70B | 2T | chat | `70B\u00b72T\u00b7chat` | **pure size** at matched budget+alignment |\n| T2 | 70B | extended (~4T or loss-plateau) | chat | `70B\u00b7ext\u00b7chat` | **pre-training sufficiency** for 70B |\n| T3 | 70B | 2T | refined-chat (extra SFT + extra RLHF round + dual RMs) | `70B\u00b72T\u00b7refined` | **alignment refinement** for 70B |\n\nCausal reads from the MVD:\n- **Size, fairly measured:** T1 vs C (matched P=2T, matched A=chat).\n- **Sufficiency:** T2 vs T1 (same size, same alignment, more tokens).\n- **Alignment refinement:** T3 vs T1 (same size, same budget, refined alignment).\n\n### 4b. Full factorial extension (ideal case \u2014 12 arms; only 6\u20138 are non-reference trains)\nAdd:\n- 13B extended budget (`13B\u00b7ext\u00b7chat`) \u2014 controls that the sufficiency effect is not size-specific.\n- 13B refined alignment (`13B\u00b7refined\u00b7chat`) \u2014 tests the **S\u00d7A interaction** (does refined alignment help 70B more than 13B?).\n- Base and SFT-only checkpoints at both sizes, 2T (`13B\u00b72T\u00b7base`, `70B\u00b72T\u00b7base`, `13B\u00b72T\u00b7sft`, `70B\u00b72T\u00b7sft`) \u2014 to decompose the alignment axis monotonically (base \u2192 SFT \u2192 chat \u2192 refined) and to give a clean \"capability before alignment\" readout.\n- Insufficient-P cell (`70B\u00b70.4T\u00b7base` and optionally `70B\u00b70.4T\u00b7chat`) \u2014 needed for the negation-mechanism test (see 4c).\n\n### 4c. The negation-mechanism comparison (explicitly flagged)\nThis is the direct operationalization of the question's \"can insufficiency negate size\":\n- **Negation test (cross-cell):** `70B\u00b7insufficientP\u00b7minimalA` (e.g., `70B\u00b70.4T\u00b7base` or `70B\u00b70.4T\u00b7sft`) **vs** `13B\u00b7sufficientP\u00b7refinedA` (e.g., `13B\u00b72T\u00b7chat` = C, or `13B\u00b7ext\u00b7chat`). If the under-trained/under-aligned 70B scores **at or below** the well-pre-trained, well-aligned 13B on capability and/or safety metrics, the negation hypothesis is supported for that factor combo.\n- The same contrast at intermediate levels (70B@2T@base vs 13B@2T@chat) isolates which single factor (P or A) is sufficient to cause the flip, and the full grid lets us map the **boundary** of the flip region.\n- This contrast is reported as its own pre-registered primary comparison, NOT pooled into the \"average size effect.\"\n\n## 5. Controls and matching discipline\n- **Primary matching = matched tokens** (T1 vs C): the fair \"size\" test. Secondary = **matched FLOPs/efficiency** (e.g., 13B@4T vs 70B@2T as an alternative \"who uses compute better\") \u2014 reported separately and never conflated with the primary readout. Both are pre-specified.\n- **Fixed recipe across all trained arms:** same tokenizer/corpus/mixture, same seed, per-size peak LR as in the paper (3.0e-4 @13B, 1.5e-4 @70B), same AdamW/cosine/warmup-2000/weight-decay-0.1/grad-clip-1.0/4M-token-batch settings, same 4096 context.\n- **Eval harness held constant:** identical prompts, few-shot lists, decoding (deterministic for benchmarks; temperature 0.6 sampling for preference evals), fixed eval seeds.\n- **Equivalence sanity check** on released reference cells vs a re-run of the same config on a small token slice, to certify that released checkpoints are acceptable stand-ins.\n- **Confounder registry:** data mixture (assumed identical), RLHF reward weighting, number of RLHF rounds per size, and 70B's extra reported safety refinement are logged per arm so any residual released-checkpoint confound is visible, never silently absorbed.\n\n## 6. Metrics \u2014 split along the question's two axes\n**(a) Pre-training sufficiency (the \"is it trained enough\" axis):**\n- Held-out validation next-token loss **vs** tokens seen (learning curve per arm), plus final loss on a fixed held-out eval split. Primary readout: **loss-gap to the 13B reference** (\u0394loss vs `13B\u00b72T` at each budget), which is the operational definition of sufficiency.\n\n**(b) Capability vs. alignment/safety (the \"does it perform AND behave\" axis):**\n- *Capability:* MMLU, HellaSwag, ARC-c, Winogrande, GSM8K, BBH, HumanEval, TruthfulQA (standard prompts, fixed few-shot).\n- *Helpfulness (alignment):* rejection-sampling win-rate on a fixed held-out prompt pool (paper-style), with a fixed LLM-as-judge rubric as the replicable proxy; refusal/preference scores reported separately from capability.\n- *Safety (alignment refinement):* taxonomy-based adversarial suite (self-harm, hate, adult content, illegal activity, PII, adversarial/safety-critical) + ToxiGen + safety-specific refusal/harmlessness rates. **Reported as a separate split**, not averaged into capability aggregates, so a safety regression or refusal-rate change in a larger model is visible rather than masked.\n\n## 7. Analysis plan\n- **Primary readouts (pre-registered):**\n  1. Size effect: \u0394(T1 \u2212 C) on capability aggregate and on safety/helpfulness splits.\n  2. Sufficiency effect: \u0394(T2 \u2212 T1) and the shape of the 70B loss curve (does 2T\u2192ext move loss and/or benchmark scores?).\n  3. Alignment effect: \u0394(T3 \u2212 T1); monotonicity across base\u2192SFT\u2192chat\u2192refined.\n  4. Negation test: cross-cell contrast (4c) with a pre-registered \"flip\" criterion (e.g., 70B-under-trained is within noise of, or below, 13B-well-trained on the primary metric).\n- **Effect-size + interaction analysis:** standardized effect sizes (Cohen's d / \u0394 in metric units) and **2-way interactions** S\u00d7P and S\u00d7A estimated from the grid; bootstrap CIs (re-sampling eval items/checkpoints); where the grid permits, a small linear model on cell means (size, log-tokens, alignment-stage) to quantify each factor's marginal contribution.\n- **Multiplicity handling:** pre-registered primary endpoints (the 4 readouts above) tested at family-wise \u03b1=0.05 (Bonferroni); all secondary benchmark/safety/split comparisons reported as effect sizes + CIs with an explicit discovery-versus-confirmation labeling; no post-hoc selection of favorable splits.\n- **Robustness:** repeat key evals with \u22652 decode seeds; report per-benchmark and per-safety-category tables, not only aggregates, so a \"negative\" overall result cannot hide a category-specific flip.\n\n## 8. Feasibility (scoping only \u2014 no run is claimed)\n**Environment note:** the provided box is CPU-only (224 cores, no GPU, network off); it can neither pre-train 70B nor run the eval arms. Feasibility is therefore delivered as arm-level budgets and staging, not as executed compute.\n\n**Approx. compute (theoretical FLOPs \u2248 6\u00b7N\u00b7D_tokens; A100-80GB \u2248 1.1e18 FLOP/hr):**\n| Arm/training piece | Tokens | FLOPs | ~A100-hrs (theoret.) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 13B reference (released; sanity check only) | 2T | 1.56e23 | ~4.5e4 (re-check) / 0 net new |\n| 70B@2T (released; sanity check only) | 2T | 8.4e23 | ~7.6e5 (paper cites ~1.7M incl. overhead) / 0 net new |\n| 70B extended (T2) | ~2T extra | +8.4e23 | +~0.8\u20131.7M |\n| 70B insufficient (0.4T) | 0.4T | 1.68e23 | ~1.5e5 |\n| Alignment per model (SFT+RLHF, \u2264 few epochs over 27.5k\u2013preference data) | n/a | ~1e21 (SFT) + small RLHF | ~1e3\u20131e4 per model |\n| 13B extended (optional) | +2T | +1.56e23 | ~1.4e5 |\n\n**MVD compute:** \u2248 13B@2T re-run sanity (small), + one 70B continuation to ~4T (dominant cost ~0.8\u20131.7M A100-hrs), + 70B alignment refinement (~1e3\u20131e4 A100-hrs), + eval of 4 arms (cheap relative to training). All of this is a scoping statement.\n**Staged plan:** Stage 0: equivalence sanity check on released checkpoints + eval harness lock-down (cheap, CPU/1\u20134 GPUs). Stage 1: run T2 (70B extended) \u2014 the only expensive pre-training continuation; checkpoint-and-evaluate every X tokens to build the loss/benchmark curve. Stage 2: run T3 alignment refinement and any SFT/base variants. Stage 3: full eval grid + analysis with pre-registered statistics. Stage 4 (ideal case only): add 13B extended/refined and insufficient-P cells to map the interaction boundary.\n**What the MVD strictly requires:** (1) the two released checkpoints (reference cells) plus an equivalence check; (2) one 70B pre-training continuation to the extended budget; (3) one 70B alignment-refinement pass; (4) a locked eval harness and the metric/analysis pipeline. If even Stage-1 compute is unavailable, a reduced design can drop T2 and keep only C, T1, T3 (size + alignment-refinement at constant P) and report the sufficiency axis as open.\n\n## 9. Uncertainties and limitations\n- Data mixture/preprocessing not public \u2192 replayed-corpus fidelity assumed, can't be verified.\n- RLHF internals not fully public \u2192 refined-alignment arm uses my operational definitions (extra SFT round, extra RLHF round, dual RMs); released 70B-chat's extra safety refinement is a confounder I log, not eliminate.\n- No source-fixed \"sufficiency\" threshold \u2192 pre-registered operational decision (loss-plateau or Chinchilla-style budget), with sensitivity analysis over the threshold.\n- Human-preference pools not public \u2192 LLM-as-judge proxy bias is a stated limitation; safety findings are benchmark-anchored (ToxiGen, taxonomy suite).\n- Interaction estimates from a sparse grid are less precise; the MVD can detect main effects and the flagged negation contrast, while the interaction-boundary map requires the full extension.\n- This is a **design only**: no training, alignment, or evaluation is claimed to have been run anywhere in this document.", "evidence": "Workspace inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (states design-only; references a track.md that is absent). find over / returned no track.md, no llama-related files, no datasets. Environment: CPU-only (224 cores), no /dev/nvidia*, network disabled, Python 3.14.4. Source-supported facts (2T corpus, per-size LR, SFT+RLHF alignment, separate helpfulness/safety RMs, 'under-trained' note) are from the public Llama-2 paper (external), used to anchor cells; they are not workspace artifacts. FLOPs and time estimates are standard 6\u00b7N\u00b7D approximations cross-checked against the paper's reported ~1.7M A100-hours for 70B@2T.", "assumptions": ["2T tokens = reference budget for both sizes; insufficiency/extended are defined relative to 2T, not to an absolute source-given threshold", "Alignment pipeline (SFT set, RM architecture/weights, RLHF procedure) transfers comparably across 13B and 70B and is separable from size", "Released checkpoints stand in for the 13B@2T and 70B@2T reference cells after a small equivalence sanity check", "'Sufficient pre-training' is operationalized by a pre-registered loss-plateau or Chinchilla-style compute threshold, with sensitivity analysis", "LLM-as-judge is a pre-registered proxy for unavailable human-preference pools; safety findings are benchmark-anchored", "Primary fair size comparison uses matched tokens; matched-FLOPs/efficiency is a secondary, separately reported comparison"], "uncertainties": ["Exact pre-training data mixture/preprocessing is not public, so corpus replay fidelity is assumed, not verifiable", "RLHF internals (helpfulness/safety reward weighting, iterations per size, 70B-specific extra safety refinement) are not fully public; this is logged